---
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
import site from "../data/site.json";
import { u } from "../lib/url.js";
---
<Base
  title="Privacy"
  eyebrow="What this site collects, and what it does not"
  dek="A static site with no account, no login and no database.">

  <div class="prose k-prose" style="max-width:var(--measure)">
    <h3>What Record Atlas itself collects</h3>
    <p><b>Nothing.</b> There is no account, no sign-in, no form and no database. The site is
    static files served by GitHub Pages. What you type into the find box, which layers you
    switch on and which year you set stay in your browser's address bar and are never sent
    anywhere.</p>

    <h3>If you choose to keep a copy online</h3>
    <p>On <a href={u("/my-research/")}>My research</a> you can keep an encrypted copy of your
    research online, with no account. The six words it gives you are made in your browser and
    are never sent to us. Your research is encrypted in your browser, and only that scrambled
    copy is sent to our storage, which cannot open it. We hold no names, no email address and no
    way to recover a copy: <b>if you lose the six words, the copy is gone</b>. Your browser keeps
    the six words (and the time of the last save) under one key, <code>ra-sync-v1</code>, so
    "Sync now" works without retyping; "Stop syncing on this device" removes it, and "Delete
    everything I have stored" removes the copy from our storage. This is off unless you switch
    it on, and the rest of the site works exactly the same without it.</p>

    <h3>If you ask Claude to read a line</h3>
    <p>On <a href={u("/read/")}>Reading the record</a> you can paste a line of a register, or photograph one, and ask Claude to draft a reading.
    <b>This is the one place where something you type leaves your browser.</b> It happens only when you press a button. The text (or the picture,
    shrunk and stripped of its hidden data in your browser) goes to our small service at <code>ai.recordatlas.org</code>, which sends it to Claude, an
    AI made by Anthropic, through Anthropic's programming interface, and returns the draft. Our service keeps <b>counts only</b>: how many tries an
    address, a six-word identity and the whole site have used today, so that nobody can run up a bill. It keeps no text, no picture, no answer and no
    name, and its request logs are off.</p>
    <p>Anthropic's published policy for this kind of use says it deletes what is sent within 30 days, keeps content that its safety systems flag
    for up to two years, and does not train its models on it without permission (<a href="https://privacy.claude.com/en/articles/7996866-how-long-do-you-store-my-organization-s-data" rel="noopener">its policy</a>;
    <a href="https://platform.claude.com/docs/en/manage-claude/api-and-data-retention" rel="noopener">its API retention page</a>). Policies change, so
    read those if it matters to you. <b>Please do not paste or photograph details of people who may be alive.</b></p>
    <p>To stop automated abuse the service asks Cloudflare's bot check (Turnstile) to confirm you are a person. That script loads only when you first
    click in the box, and Cloudflare sees your address and browser as it does for any site that uses it. If you have set up sync, your six words give the
    service a separate identity for your allowance; the service cannot open your saved research with it. Everything else on that page, the word finder
    and the date decoder, runs in your browser and sends nothing.</p>

    <h3>What your browser fetches from elsewhere</h3>
    <ul>
      <li><b>Map tiles</b> from Esri (the default map, with its place names, and the satellite
      view) or OpenTopoMap (the terrain view), depending on which basemap is showing. Those
      services see your IP address and which tiles you asked for, as any map on the web
      does.</li>
      <li><b>Leaflet</b>, the mapping library, from the Cloudflare CDN.</li>
      <li><b>Fonts</b> from Google Fonts.</li>
      <li><b>GitHub Pages</b> serves the site and keeps standard server logs.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>Following a link to a provider takes you to that provider's site, under their terms
    and not these.</p>

    <h3>Advertising</h3>
    {site.ads.enabled ? (
      <>
        <p>This site carries a single advertisement in its footer, served by Google AdSense.
        Google and its partners may use cookies to serve and measure ads. In the EEA, the UK
        and Switzerland, Google's own consent message appears before <b>personalised</b> ads
        are served, and you can change that choice at any time.</p>
        {/* THIS PARAGRAPH USED TO CLAIM MORE THAN IS TRUE. It said the ad
            script "is not loaded at all until you have given consent", which
            would need a consent-management platform this site does not run.
            What actually happens is that Google's own GDPR message, enabled
            in the AdSense dashboard, gates personalisation. That is
            compliant, and it is a different mechanism — and a privacy page
            is the last place to describe a protection a reader does not
            actually have. */}
        <p>You can review Google's practices at
        <a href="https://policies.google.com/technologies/partner-sites" rel="noopener">
        policies.google.com/technologies/partner-sites</a>.</p>
      </>
    ) : (
      <p>This site carries no advertising at present. If that changes, a single unit will
      appear in the footer, it will be served by Google AdSense, and in the EEA, the UK and
      Switzerland nothing will load until you have consented through a banner. This page will
      say so before it happens.</p>
    )}

    <h3>Counting visits</h3>
    {site.analytics?.goatcounter ? (
      <>
        <p>This site counts page views with
        <a href="https://www.goatcounter.com/" rel="noopener">GoatCounter</a>, the same
        counter every archive in this family of sites uses. It sets <b>no cookies</b>, gives
        you no identifier, and keeps no personal data — it records that a page was loaded,
        roughly where in the world from, and which page sent you. Nothing is shared with any
        advertiser, and nothing is joined to the advertising above.</p>
        <p>There is no consent banner for it because there is nothing to consent to: it
        collects no personal data in the first place. If you would rather not be counted at
        all, any tracker blocker will stop it, and the site works identically without it.</p>
      </>
    ) : (
      <p>This site does not count visits.</p>
    )}

    <h3>Living people</h3>
    <p>This map is about <b>records and the institutions that hold them</b>, not about people.
    It carries no names of individuals, living or dead. The one-family archive it grew out of
    keeps a strict rule about living people; this site sidesteps the question by never holding
    a person in the first place.</p>

    <h3>Getting in touch</h3>
    <p>Corrections, including anything you want removed, go through
    <a href={site.repo + "/issues"}>the public issue tracker</a>.</p>
  </div>
</Base>

<style>.prose h3 { margin-top: 28px; } .prose ul { font-size: 14px; line-height: 1.7; }</style>
